window.docContents[224].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/#chapter-02___section-2-1":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory","header":"2.1. What is representation theory?","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/#chapter-02___section-2-1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Convention___-algebra-means-associative-algebra-with-unit/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Proposition2___2___3":{"contents":"From now on, by an algebra A we will mean an associative algebra with a unit.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Convention: algebra means associative algebra with unit","header":"Convention: algebra means associative algebra with unit","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Convention___-algebra-means-associative-algebra-with-unit/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Proposition2___2___3"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Path-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver-has-a-unit/#Chapter2___Remark2___8___5":{"contents":"Remark 2.8.5. It is easy to see that if Q is a finite set then \\sum_{i \\in I} p_i = 1, so P_Q is an algebra with unit.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Path algebra of a finite quiver has a unit","header":"Path algebra of a finite quiver has a unit","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Path-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver-has-a-unit/#Chapter2___Remark2___8___5"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products--Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2--Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A tensor-product equivalence respecting both displayed factor actions induces equivalences of both factors.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]\n\nIf the tensor product of the Jacobson-radical quotients is semisimple, the Jacobson radical of\nthe tensor product is contained in the third auxiliary ideal.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]\n\nFor two algebra actions on a finite-dimensional vector space over an algebraically closed field,\nthe Jacobson radical of the tensor product of their ranges equals the corresponding third\nauxiliary ideal.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]\n\nFor finite-dimensional algebras over an algebraically closed field, the Jacobson radical of\ntheir tensor product equals the third auxiliary ideal.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]\n\nThe quotient of a finite-dimensional tensor-product algebra by its Jacobson radical is algebra-equivalent to the tensor product of the two Jacobson-radical quotients.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.10. Representations of tensor products\u0009Proof of Theorem 3.10.2\u0009Proof of Theorem 3.10.2\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products--Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2--Proof-of-Theorem-3___10___2--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras/#Chapter3___Problem3___9___3":{"contents":"Problem 3.9.3. Let Q be a quiver without oriented cycles, and let P_Q be the path algebra of Q. Find irreducible representations of P_Q and compute \\operatorname{Ext}^1 between them. Classify 2-dimensional representations of P_Q.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.9. Problems\u0009Irreducible representations and Ext^1 for path algebras","header":"Irreducible representations and Ext^1 for path algebras","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras/#Chapter3___Problem3___9___3"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___11___-Historical-interlude___-Georg-Frobenius___s-___Principle-of-Horse-Trade___/Section-4___11___-Historical-interlude-___-Georg-Frobenius___s-Principle-of-Horse-Trade/#Chapter4___Discussion_4___11":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.11. Historical interlude: Georg Frobenius's \"Principle of Horse Trade\"\u0009Section 4.11: Historical interlude — Georg Frobenius's Principle of Horse Trade","header":"Section 4.11: Historical interlude — Georg Frobenius's Principle of Horse Trade","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___11___-Historical-interlude___-Georg-Frobenius___s-___Principle-of-Horse-Trade___/Section-4___11___-Historical-interlude-___-Georg-Frobenius___s-Principle-of-Horse-Trade/#Chapter4___Discussion_4___11"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Proof-of-Proposition-5___14___1/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Proposition5___14___1":{"contents":"Proof. By Lemmas 5.13.3 and 5.13.4,\n\n\\operatorname{Hom}(U_\\lambda, V_\\mu) = \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{C}[S_n]a_\\lambda, \\mathbb{C}[S_n]a_\\mu b_\\mu) = a_\\lambda \\mathbb{C}[S_n] a_\\mu b_\\mu,\n\nand the result follows from Lemmas 5.13.1 and 5.13.2. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\u0009Proof of Proposition 5.14.1","header":"Proof of Proposition 5.14.1","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Proof-of-Proposition-5___14___1/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Proposition5___14___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/Section-5___25___-Representations-of-GL_2_LPAR_F_q_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___25":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\u0009Section 5.25: Representations of GL_2(F_q)","header":"Section 5.25: Representations of GL_2(F_q)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/Section-5___25___-Representations-of-GL_2_LPAR_F_q_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___25"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers--Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_--Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A finite group character value is a sum of complex roots whose orders divide the group cardinality.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nA finite product of squared norms of character values is integral over the integers.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nThe product of character values over nonidentity elements, paired with their inverses, is rational.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nA complex ring endomorphism that acts as a fixed power on the relevant roots sends character values to values on the corresponding group powers.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nA ring endomorphism acting as a coprime power on the relevant roots fixes the character pairing product over nonidentity elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nRaising elements of a group to an exponent coprime to its cardinality is bijective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nComposing a function with a coprime power map does not change its product over the nonidentity elements of a finite group.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\nA rational number whose complex image is integral over the integers cannot lie strictly between zero and one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.2.8; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\u0009Modification of vanishing argument without part (a)\u0009Modification of vanishing argument without part (a)\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers--Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_--Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation--Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_--Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For a subgroup-invariant complex-valued function, the normalized sum over the group equals the sum over chosen quotient representatives.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.9.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.9.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.9. The Frobenius formula for the character of an induced representation\u0009Proof of Theorem 5.9.1 (Frobenius formula)\u0009Proof of Theorem 5.9.1 (Frobenius formula)\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation--Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_--Proof-of-Theorem-5___9___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-formula_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/#chapter-09___section-9-2":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras","header":"9.2. Projective covers","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/#chapter-09___section-9-2"}});